---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import DataTable from '../components/DataTable.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Community"
  description="How to participate in UniDPP: contributing to the open repositories, intellectual-property rights and the standards track, governance, and the steward organizations."
  eyebrow="Adopt and participate"
>
  <h1>Community</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    UniDPP is developed in the open, and participation is designed to be simple: file
    findings, open pull requests, or carry the work through your standards member body.
    No contributor agreement stands between you and the code.
  </p>

  <section aria-labelledby="contributing-h">
    <h2 id="contributing-h">Contributing</h2>
    <p>
      Everything lives in the open at
      <a href="https://github.com/unidpp">github.com/unidpp</a> — the Rust core, the four
      language SDKs, the resolver, trust, and explorer repositories, the specification
      sources, the terminology dataset, and this website. The working rhythm:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Issues before code.</strong> File what you found — a defect in the EN
        corpus, a spec ambiguity, a build break — with the citation or command that
        reproduces it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Pull requests against the source.</strong> This site renders the
        specification snapshot (<code>src/spec/</code>) and the papers; corrections to
        factual pages, the comparison table, and the sitemap are all ordinary pull
        requests.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Evidence over assertion.</strong> The project's register is factual:
        findings cite the standard's own normative text, and demo traces are generated,
        not transcribed. Contributions in the same register land fastest.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Licences stay permissive.</strong> Apache-2.0 or MIT per repository,
        CC-BY-4.0 for the specification. Contributions are accepted under the terms of the
        repository they land in; there is no CLA and no copyright assignment.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="ipr" class="plate" aria-labelledby="ipr-h">
    <h2 id="ipr-h">IPR and the standards track</h2>
    <p>
      The standards work is carried in ISO/IEC JTC&nbsp;5 and partner bodies — ISO/TC 154,
      OIML, UN/CEFACT, and CEN where the European profile requires it — and contributed
      through national member bodies and liaison channels. The project does not create a
      rival standards track: papers are submitted as N-documents through the secretariat,
      and new work items (NWIP-1, NWIP-2) follow the ISO process with Forms 4 drafted
      from the plan and the audit evidence. Essential-claims licensing follows the rules
      of the body in which each work item runs; the open repositories predate any work
      item and remain Apache-2.0 / MIT.
    </p>
    <p>
      If your organization is a member of a steward body, the practical entry point is
      your national member body's mirror committee; the catalogue of planned
      contributions is on the <a href="/papers/">papers page</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="governance" class="plate" aria-labelledby="governance-h">
    <h2 id="governance-h">Governance</h2>
    <p>
      No single organization owns the framework: each steward body is responsible for the
      layer it already operates as an international standards activity.
    </p>
    <DataTable
      columns={['Body', 'Responsibility']}
      rows={[
        ['<strong>CalConnect</strong>', 'Project home; terminology; time, events, and the state machine; offline freshness; SIGNATIF with TC 154; the UnitsML quantity chain.'],
        ['<strong>ISO/TC 154</strong>', 'Identity; trade-documented seams; registry governance with UN/CEFACT; issuance rules; the NWIP vehicle.'],
        ['<strong>OIML</strong>', 'Measurement validity and decision rules; CNML/ESDC; issuance-role governance; the first vertical pilot; the twin ladder.'],
        ['<strong>ELF</strong>', 'The EXPRESS core; seam contracts (S3/S8); the mapping technical report; the AP242 identity-lattice lineage; OpenCDD dictionary technology.'],
        ['<strong>Enosema</strong>', 'FERIN registry operations; ISO 19135:2025 expressibility guarantees.'],
        ['<strong>UnitsML coalition</strong>', 'The quantity chain: ISO 80000 → UnitsDB/UnitsML → OpenCDD.'],
      ]}
    />
    <p>
      Guardrails the project holds to are stated on the
      <a href="/about/">about page</a>: the EN series' own neutrality language enforced
      rather than amended, no rival central registry, one canonical units chain, one
      registry, one trust framework.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="adopt" class="plate" aria-labelledby="adopt-h">
    <h2 id="adopt-h">Adopting</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Try the stack</strong> — the quickstart and the eight repositories are on
        the <a href="/implementation/">implementation page</a>; the explorer runs in your
        browser with live WebCrypto.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Run the evidence</strong> — the
        <a href="/implementation/demos/">demonstrations</a> and the
        <a href="/implementation/conformance/">conformance register</a> are reproducible
        from a clean clone.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Read the specification</strong> — all five parts, working-draft stage, on
        the <a href="/specs/">specification pages</a>.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="contact" aria-labelledby="contact-h">
    <h2 id="contact-h">Contact</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>GitHub: <a href="https://github.com/unidpp">github.com/unidpp</a></li>
      <li>Email: <a href="mailto:contact@unidpp.org">contact@unidpp.org</a></li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
